    "raw": "# Gpubox Security & Content Security Policy (CSP) Guide\n\nThis guide details the security model, Content Security Policy (CSP) directives, and safe evaluation patterns for running Gpubox in production applications.\n\n## Content Security Policy Requirements\n\nWhen integrating Gpubox into browser applications, the following CSP directives apply depending on the execution engine:\n\n### 1. WebAssembly Engine (Analytical Core)\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; object-src 'none';\n```\n- The WebAssembly engine compiles and executes pre-built WASM binaries without requiring JavaScript code generation.\n- Modern browsers allow WASM compilation with `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` without needing `'unsafe-eval'`.\n\n### 2. JavaScript Transpiler & Dynamic JIT Engine\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval'; object-src 'none';\n```\n- The client-side CUDA C++ transpiler transforms CUDA syntax into JavaScript generator functions and executes them via `new Function('__rt', code)`.\n- If your application enforces a strict CSP forbidding `'unsafe-eval'`, you must isolate dynamic CUDA execution into a **Sandboxed Web Worker**.\n\n## Sandboxed Web Worker Isolation (Zero Main-Thread CSP Risk)\n\nTo safely execute untrusted student or user CUDA code in enterprise environments:\n\n1. Create a dedicated Web Worker on a separate origin or sandboxed subdomain.\n2. Grant `'unsafe-eval'` or `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` exclusively to the worker thread.\n3. Keep your main application UI strictly protected under `default-src 'self'`.\n\n```javascript\n// main.js\nconst worker = new Worker('/workers/gpubox-sandbox.js');\nworker.postMessage({ type: 'RUN', code: userCode });\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {\n  renderResults(e.data.result);\n};\n```\n\n## Watchdog & Resource Limits\n\nTo prevent runaway user loops or denial-of-service from infinite loops:\n- Gpubox enforces an internal step watchdog (default: 50,000 steps).\n- It checks time limits (default: 2,000 ms).\n- It enforces strict virtual VRAM capacity checks (e.g. 80 GiB per device) without allocating real host physical memory.\n",
    "html": "<h1>Gpubox Security &amp; Content Security Policy (CSP) Guide</h1>\n<p>This guide details the security model, Content Security Policy (CSP) directives, and safe evaluation patterns for running Gpubox in production applications.</p>\n<h2>Content Security Policy Requirements</h2>\n<p>When integrating Gpubox into browser applications, the following CSP directives apply depending on the execution engine:</p>\n<h3>1. WebAssembly Engine (Analytical Core)</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\">Content-Security-Policy: default-src &#39;self&#39;; script-src &#39;self&#39; &#39;wasm-unsafe-eval&#39;; object-src &#39;none&#39;;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The WebAssembly engine compiles and executes pre-built WASM binaries without requiring JavaScript code generation.</li>\n<li>Modern browsers allow WASM compilation with <code>&#39;wasm-unsafe-eval&#39;</code> without needing <code>&#39;unsafe-eval&#39;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. JavaScript Transpiler &amp; Dynamic JIT Engine</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\">Content-Security-Policy: default-src &#39;self&#39;; script-src &#39;self&#39; &#39;unsafe-eval&#39;; object-src &#39;none&#39;;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The client-side CUDA C++ transpiler transforms CUDA syntax into JavaScript generator functions and executes them via <code>new Function(&#39;__rt&#39;, code)</code>.</li>\n<li>If your application enforces a strict CSP forbidding <code>&#39;unsafe-eval&#39;</code>, you must isolate dynamic CUDA execution into a <strong>Sandboxed Web Worker</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sandboxed Web Worker Isolation (Zero Main-Thread CSP Risk)</h2>\n<p>To safely execute untrusted student or user CUDA code in enterprise environments:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a dedicated Web Worker on a separate origin or sandboxed subdomain.</li>\n<li>Grant <code>&#39;unsafe-eval&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;wasm-unsafe-eval&#39;</code> exclusively to the worker thread.</li>\n<li>Keep your main application UI strictly protected under <code>default-src &#39;self&#39;</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">// main.js\nconst worker = new Worker(&#39;/workers/gpubox-sandbox.js&#39;);\nworker.postMessage({ type: &#39;RUN&#39;, code: userCode });\nworker.onmessage = (e) =&gt; {\n  renderResults(e.data.result);\n};\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Watchdog &amp; Resource Limits</h2>\n<p>To prevent runaway user loops or denial-of-service from infinite loops:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gpubox enforces an internal step watchdog (default: 50,000 steps).</li>\n<li>It checks time limits (default: 2,000 ms).</li>\n<li>It enforces strict virtual VRAM capacity checks (e.g. 80 GiB per device) without allocating real host physical memory.</li>\n</ul>\n"
